{"answer": "The critic's case assumes without proof that the unfiltered graph's grounded extension is the intended baseline and that the target was required to enlarge that exact set. But the problem demands expanding or strengthening DeepReason's grounded extension relative to its prior capability, not relative to an arbitrary unfiltered relation. The priority filter changes which attacks are active at the fixpoint; accepted conjectures are then computed over the filtered graph. Consequently, the resulting grounded extension is not a subset of the unfiltered one in any sense the critic establishes: deleting an attack on B can allow B (and arguments defended only by B) to be accepted, while deleting an attack whose removal is irrelevant cannot. The critic provides no lattice-theoretic or graphical proof that the filtered grounded extension is set-contained in the unfiltered one for all priority assignments; the monotone-deletion premise does not by itself establish this, because grounded acceptance depends on attack incidence at targets, not merely on attack-set cardinality. The critic also concedes that root validity is preserved, then mischaracterizes preservation as 'not adding acceptance'; but retaining top-priority roots as accepted where the unfiltered graph might have admitted attacks against them is not merely preserving an already-derived property unless the critic proves those attacks were already ineffective. No such proof is given. The critic additionally does not address the target's stated dependence on the configured total preorder: with committed roots at unique top priority, attacks against them are filtered out, which can alter the grounded extension's composition, not just its size. The weakness the critic might exploit is the target's own admitted limitation that priority is exogenous and unchallengeable, but that is a scope caveat, not a demonstration that the transformation fails the problem as written. Absent an established subset relation between filtered and unfiltered grounded extensions, the claim that the proposal 'can only shrink' acceptance is unproven, and the failure verdict does not follow. The critic has not met the burden of showing that no priority assignment can yield a grounded extension that expands or strengthens DeepReason's accepted-conjecture set; a single counterexample would refute the universal shrinkage claim, and none of the critic's clauses rules such a case out."}